    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "31-34",
      "detail": "3rd in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "The Blue Jays are still living in the margins - good enough to stay relevant, not yet good enough to breathe easy. <strong>Saturday's 6-4 win over Baltimore</strong> snapped the latest wobble and gave Toronto a much-needed jolt after a 13-3 loss the night before, a tidy reminder that this club still has some punch when the bats wake up. At 31-34, sitting third in the AL East and 8 games back, they are not chasing the leaders so much as trying to claw their way back into the conversation, with a -1.5 points mark and the No. 8 playoff seed hanging there like a warning sign. Ernie Clement supplied the big swing with a three-run homer, and Brandon Valenzuela chipped in three hits and an RBI - exactly the sort of scrappy production Toronto needs while it waits for steadier offense. The rotation and lineup have both had their say in the past four days, trading haymakers with Atlanta and Baltimore, but the record says the same thing every morning: urgency, not comfort. <strong>Now comes Sunday's 12:37 p.m. ET rematch with the Orioles, and Toronto needs that winning edge to stick.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated June 7, 2026"
      },
