      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Commanders",
        "body": "Spring football has a way of telling the truth before anybody wants to hear it. The Commanders are in that stage now - no pads, no excuses, just auditions - and <strong>the roster squeeze is already the story, with Adam Peters trimming and tweaking the depth chart after signing TE Anthony Firkser and releasing WR Ja'Corey Brooks on June 2</strong>. Washington sits 3rd in the NFC East at 6 games behind, with a 1-game winning streak and the awkward stamp of a 14th-place playoff seed, which says plenty about where the rebuild stands: not broken, but not settled either. The real intrigue is at the margins, where young receivers, tight ends, and defensive depth pieces are trying to force their way onto a crowded board while the staff sorts out who fits and who's just taking up air. That is the grind of OTAs and minicamp - new faces learning the language, veterans protecting their turf, and no one getting a free pass. <strong>What matters next is whether the newcomers can stick and whether the draft class starts flashing enough to turn a crowded summer into a real September rotation.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated June 7, 2026"
      },
